# Ethos-U Profile and Smoke Test

The `geisa-imx93` image selects the NXP Ethos-U runtime and the
`geisa-nxp-ethosu-tflite-profile` package. The profile is generated during the
build at:

    /platform/profiles/nxp-ethosu-tflite

It contains the private TensorFlow Lite runtime, Ethos-U delegate and runtime
libraries, NumPy, and PyAV needed by the profile loader. The image also provides
the Ethos-U driver stack, firmware, Vela, device permissions, and the
`tensorflow-lite-ethosu-delegate` package. The device used by the runtime is
`/dev/ethosu0`.

The profile is selected by the `geisa-imx93` system configuration. It is not a
checked-in archive. The build assembles it from Yocto runtime outputs and a
checksum-pinned PyAV wheel.

## Smoke Test

`geisa-ethosu-smoke-test` is a platform/enabler qualification command. It is
not a GEISA application, managed application, or performance benchmark.

Run the image-level check with the system runtime:

    geisa-ethosu-smoke-test

Run through the generated profile when qualifying the profile itself:

    geisa-ethosu-smoke-test --profile /platform/profiles/nxp-ethosu-tflite

The helper checks the supplied TensorFlow Lite model checksum, compiles a
temporary model with Vela for `ethos-u65-256`, loads the Ethos-U delegate, and
runs the Grace Hopper image example through `/dev/ethosu0`. A successful run
requires the expected inference result and a delegate message showing at least
one delegated graph node. This confirms delegated inference rather than device
presence, firmware startup, or CPU-only execution.

The command prints the model and compiled-model checksums, delegation evidence,
the inference result, and an explicit final status:

    PASS: Ethos-U accelerated inference verified

Generated files are removed on exit. The command does not require network
access or starting of an LXC container.

Failures end with `FAIL:` and include the captured diagnostic output. The
command is intended as a simple check the NPU is operational and usable and
not intended as any form of performance benchmark.

The generated profile combines NXP runtime components with the PyAV wheel and
bundled multimedia libraries. See [the image licensing notes](https://github.com/geisa/community/tree/main/platforms/nxp-imx9-yocto/docs/licensing.md)
for the package and redistribution review required before distributing an image
that includes it.
